1 OCCU Grain Dealer, businessman, cotton goods manufacturer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} FRANK ELISHA SPRAGUE was born in Killingly, Conn., on November 5, 1850, but was, while in college, and has since been, a resident of Providence, R. I. He was the son of Samuel Stearns and Esther Pierce (Hutchings) 
2 CONC and prepared for college at a private school in Providence. He writes that the panic struck him broadside just as he was leaving harbor, and that he has had headwinds and counter currents ever since. He went to Chicago in the autu
2 CONC mn of 1873 to learn the grain business, and while there attended his first and only Alumni reunion. He remained in Chicago six months and spent another half year roaming about the West, in a vain search for a promising business op
2 CONC ening. In the summer of 1874 he returned to Providence and engaged in cotton-manufacturing. He says he worked like a dog for four years, superintending the mill, etc., but sold out his interest a few months ago and has now joined 
2 CONC the family concern of Samuel S. Sprague & Co., consisting of Samuel S., Charles H. and Henry S. Sprague, dealers in flour and grain in Providence. Frank concludes he has now found smooth waters and fair sailing. He has well define
2 CONC d matrimonial intentions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Address: Care of S. S. Sprague & Co., Providence, R. I.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=phwEAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA69&lpg=PA64&ots=vF4W-18W0a&dq=%22Frank+Elisha+Sprague%22+1850&output=text#c_top
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliographic information QR code for A biographical record Title	A biographical record Author	Yale University. Class of 1873 Published	1880 Original from	Harvard University Digitized	Jun 29, 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT FRANK ELISHA SPRAGUE 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antecedents — Son of Samuel Sterns Sprague and Esther Pierce  Hutchins. Samuel S. Sprague, a wholesale grain dealer, spent his busi-  ness life in Providence, but was born in South Killingly, Windham  county, Conn., where his fath
2 CONC er, Elisha Leavens Sprague, was a farmer.  Three and perhaps four generations of the family began life on the same  farm there. Mrs. Samuel S. Sprague was a daughter of Simon Hutchins,  also of Windham county. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life — Born in South Killingly, Conn., November 5, 1850, but  lived in Providence from 1851 and there prepared for college in a pri-  vate school. 
2 CONT
2 CONT College honors, etc. — Delta Kappa, Phi Theta Psi, Psi Upsilon. A  first dispute at junior exhibition and also at Commencement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After career — Having spent most of the year following graduation  at various points in the west, he returned to Providence and was employed  in manufacturing during the succeeding ten years or more. In 1885,  however, he removed 
2 CONC to Minneapolis and became engaged in the invest-  ment brokerage business, which he still pursues. His letterhead describes  him as a "dealer in mortgages, notes, and local securities" at No. 7  South Fifth street. He writes : " M
2 CONC y life has moved along in an un-  eventful way without drawing from my fellow citizens any particular  demonstration favorable or unfavorable. I have had good health, reason-  able income for modest wants, and a fairly clear consc
2 CONC ience. ' ' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family — Married in Pittsfield, Mass., February 10, 1887, Maria  Talcott Lane of that place. Children : A son who died at birth, June  23, 1889; Esther, born March 15, 1892; John Lane, born August 21,  1895. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Address — 7 South Fifth street, Minneapolis, Minn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archive.org/stream/cu31924030632552/cu31924030632552_djvu.txt
2 CONT
