2 CONT
2 CONT From letters that my great-Uncle sent from Norway when he visited in 1965, Martin and Peder came together to Minnesota in 1872 (This is incorrect as Peder was married in Minnesota in March of 1872, and had first child there in Dec
2 CONC . of 1872.  He was also listed in 1870 Census).  They lived in Ostre Gran, part of the large Gran Gaard (farm) up the hill from Lena in the County of Toten about 50 miles north of Oslo.  From a letter  dated Aug. 25, 1965:
2 CONT
2 CONT      For the family tree search we logically started in the town of Gran in Hadeland.  No Grans there and no record of any.  But we did hear of the Gran Gaard (farm) in the next county north; Toten.  I remember my father mentionin
2 CONC g Toten.  So we took the train to Lena (the nearest town and a taxi to the Gaarden Gran and knocked on the door of the large elegant mansion.  The present owners, Mr. and Mrs. Ole Nokleby, invited us in for coffee and a look aroun
2 CONC d. (I snapped pictures of a map of Gran on the wall - one enclosed).  We bacame friends right away and they invited us to stay several days.  They had plenty of room in the huge house - for instance, the dining room is about 25' X
2 CONC  40' and seated comfortably over 100 people for a dinner party, about which more later.
2 CONT      Ole's uncle, Hans Nokleby, a very fine educated old gentleman, who has another huge mansion ( also eleantly furnished with priceless antiques - reminds me of Mt. Vernon) entertained us one evening, and gave us the history of 
2 CONC Gran ( and our family) back to 1300!  When it is translated I'll send you a copy.
2 CONT      One day we went by bus to Hamar to see the govt. archives - church, school, etc. records from way back.  We happened to find (we had so little to start with, that the pike (girl)caretaker of the books said it was hopeless).  
2 CONC Entries for Peder Anton Poulsen (born Feb. 15, 1848 and Martin Poulsen (born July 31, 1854) - both of Gran.  By then it was 3 PM and closing time, so we reluctantly left.  But the name Poul Andreas was enough - Many people in Tote
2 CONC n remembered him ( he died apparently about 1896, in a house we visited ).  But they knew of only one child, Juliane.  We told them of at least 2 sons in the Hamar records, and they were surprised.  It turned out that there were 1
2 CONC 0 children, just as I had heard from my Aunts, 3 boys, and 7 girls, - but all left around 100 years ago for America or Australia except Juliane, the eldest ( born Dec. 25, 1837, died June 4, 1931 according to her tombstone in Kolb
2 CONC er Church yard).  She married Martin Odegard, so all our relatives now in Norway are descended from Juliane Odegard.  We visited Juliane's daughter, Josefine, 93 who seemed to have a good memory - she said without prompting, that 
2 CONC her mother had 3 brothers & 6 sisters - all had left Norway.  She gave me a picture of her grandparents, Poul Andreas and Ann Karine, apparently enlarged from an old tin-type.  I took the glass off and photographed it. 
2 CONT      I wrote to Miss Edna McDonald 501 "Claridge", 30 Flinders ST., Darlinghurst, Sydney Australia, telling her what we had found.  She confirmed some names and dates, and thanked me for the additional data we had uncovered.  Edna
2 CONC  (I have never met her) is descedned from my Grandfather's brother, who emigrated to Australia.  But Edna did throw one monkey wrench into the deal- she said her Grandfather's name was Peter Anton Gran (Changed to Grahn in Austral
2 CONC ia).  And my father and Aunts had told me that my Grandfather was Peter.  But in Norway Peder and Peter are 2 different names, and some families have both.  In English, both are Peter, of course.  I am trying to solve that one now
2 CONC .
2 CONT      About names - until a law in 1921, very few Norwegians had surnames, as we have.  Locally, a son of Poul might be Ole Poulsen; a daughter might be Oline Poulsdatter.  But when they travelled, they added the name of their farm
2 CONC  for identification.  So don't get thrown (as I was at first by the Poul Andreas Jensen, Peder Anton Poulsen, etc.
2 CONT      Here is a fairly accurate list of the children of Poul and Ann Karine, except, amazingly, my own grandfather's name:
2 CONT
2 CONT Per (??) - Minnesota, then Seattle
2 CONT Peder Anton - Australia
2 CONT Martin - Buckley, Wash.
2 CONT Juliane - stayed in Norway (Odegard)
2 CONT Holja - USA (Hanson)
2 CONT Jorgine - USA (Bjerke)
2 CONT Marta - USA
2 CONT Klara - USA
2 CONT Anna ? - USA
2 CONT Julia? - USA
2 CONT
2 CONT      One of Juliane's granddaughters showed me a letter from a descendant of Marta, a Mrs. Lloyd Henderson, Victory Wisconsin.  I wrote to her and hope she can contribute something.
2 CONT      Apparently Poul Andreas never owned all of Gran, although his ancestors did.  He owned Ostre Gran, the eastern part on the map.  The rest of Gran was owned by Armaud [sp?] Boe and then his son Per Boe - one of the few familie
2 CONC s that had a permanent surname.  But Poul Andreas liked the effects of snaps and other beverages too well, and he wasn't afraid to put up his horses, cattle, etc. as stakes in a "friendly" card game when his cash ran out.  Things 
2 CONC at Ostre Gran were going down when the 9 children left, and when they were no longer there to help out, Ostre Gran went to Per Boe, making him the richest man on the biggest farm (Gran) in Toten.  Poul Andreas finished his days in
2 CONC  a small house about a mile from Gran.
2 CONT      Oh yes, Edna Mc Donald said Martin (see p. 3) has a grandson in Wash. D.C.  He is 57-60 years old and with the State Dept. - in the Consular Corps.  His name is Robert Gran (Apparently now changed to Grover) Cleveland.  How a
2 CONC bout looking him up and seeing if he has any more dope on the family - and telling him what we have found.
2 CONT      To continue our story - while at the Gran gaard, we became such good friends with the Noklebys that they insisted we return for the confirmation of their eldest son, Honannes.  (They are trying to change their 2 sons names le
2 CONC gally to Gran from Nokleby - won't thaat louse up the efforts of family tree searchers 100 years from now!).  On Aug. 15, and in Norway, a confirmation is a big event, like a wedding.  So we went back for a week (overnight boat to
2 CONC  Oslo, 2 hr. train ride to Eina, 1/2 hr bus ride to Lena, taxi to Gran Garden).  Sunday was the big day - about 40 young people confirmed in kolbu church - each had a big party.  Over 100 people were at Johannes party, plus about 
2 CONC 15 cooking and serving the fancy dinner.  Many skols, songs, speaches and lots of food, beer, snaps.  It was well worth seeing......
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 						Elbow Lake, Minnesota.
2 CONT 						October 20, 1941
2 CONT
2 CONT Florence Gran Smith
2 CONT 401 S. Olive, L.A.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mrs. Smith;  Have yours of the 13th at hand which had been written to John Drubeon, Clerk of Elk Lake Township, pertaining to birth records of certain members of your family.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have no record of Henry P. Gran.  We have that of Emily Gran, but the first name is spelled Emila and the parents names are not given; we have that of Amanda Gran but the first name is spelled Emanda and parents names are not g
2 CONC iven; that of Adolph Martin Gran is listed as Peder M. A. Gran and parents names are spelled Peder and Egness; there is a record of Florence Sophia Gran but the date of birth is listed as April 16, 1885 and parents names are Peder
2 CONC  and Agnete; the one of Anna M. Gran is listed as Albert and parents names are Peder and Agnesa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now there is no question but that these are the records for which you are looking but there are a number of mistakes as you can readily see.  How this happened we do not know but it may be that they were turned in incorrectly, how
2 CONC ever, we have blanks that could be sent you in order to have them corrected.  You state that you have an Uncle who could sign affidavits and that he lives in Los Angeles.  If you so desire I will send you blanks to have these birt
2 CONC hs corrected so that they shall appear as they should.  There is no charge for this.  If you wish certified copies, the fee is 50 cents for each one.  If you wish to have the record of Henry P. Gran established, it could be arrang
2 CONC ed.  The fee for establishing a birth is $1.00.  So, if you want this record established and certified copies of each and every record, the fee is $4.00.  That also includes necessary corrections that need to be made.
2 CONT
2 CONT Let me know and I will send you the blanks if you want them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very Truly Yours,
2 CONT 						Walter R. Summers
2 CONT 						Clerk of the District Court,
2 CONT 						Elbow Lake, Minn.
