3 CITY Seattle
3 STAE Washington
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kilder  1.[S3] Lærdal Bygdebok II, Espe, Alfred - Hovland, Kåre, (Lærdal Kommune, 1990), side 222 og See The family story «Lysnes of Laerdal» p 51 + MyHeritage
2 CONT
2 CONT  When Maddie was a child, she had a piece of her ear cut off by an arrow during an Indian raid.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Burial record and obituary:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/74297308/metta-solberg
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Louis Solberg 
2 CONT
2 CONT METHA (MATTIE) LYSNE SOLBERG
2 CONT
2 CONT Metha Oldsdatter was born in Laerdal, Sogn og Fjordane County, Norway, on February 19, 1848. She was baptized in the Tonjums Church of the Laerdal Parish on April 30, 1848. There are several discrepancies as to her date of birth, 
2 CONC with the birth record from the Laerdal Parish assumed to be the correct date. Although her birth record shows her name as Metha, she always went by the name “Mattie” in later life. Her parents were Ole Tomasson Lysne and Anna Selt
2 CONC hun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She emigrated to the United States in 1856 along with her parents and brothers and sisters. The Laerdal Parish Records state that over 140 people left the parish on April 16, 1856, bound for “Amerika”. They first settled in Adams 
2 CONC County, Wisconsin. She is listed in the 1860 federal census as living with her parents in Monroe Township, Adams County. The census, which lists her name as “Martha”, shows that she is eleven years old and that she has attended sc
2 CONC hool within the year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, the family moved to Brown County, Minnesota, where they established residence on a farm about seven miles west of New Ulm, along the Big Cottonwood River. Two of her brothers, Ole and Knut had come here two years prior an
2 CONC d had “squatted” on a piece of property hoping to homestead it later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Here the family began the life of the settlers, working hard clearing land and growing whatever crops possible to provide for the family. But life was about to change. During the simmer of 1862, the Sioux Indian uprising took plac
2 CONC e and drove the family from their farm into New Ulm where they took refuge with other settlers. Amidst all of the fright and confusion, Mattie, who had gone to the spring for water on that fateful day, became lost and was taken to
2 CONC  shelter by a German family. Word has it that she was not reunited with her family for an entire year. Because of the feared danger posed by the Indians, the family was forced to leave New Ulm with other residents and eventually m
2 CONC ade their way to Goodhue County where they are said to have spent the winter living with another family. Conditions were very meager for the family at this time as they had lost most of their possessions during the flight from the
2 CONC ir farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1865 Minnesota census lists her name as “Melthe” and living with her parents in Holden Township, Goodhue County. The 1870 federal census shows the family, with her brother, Ole, as the head, now living in Warsaw Township in Go
2 CONC odhue County. It lists “Matty” age 22 and living at home along with her two younger sisters and parents.  Her father, Ole, is shown as 80 years which is somewhat in conflict with his birth year of 1797. Her mother, Anna, is shown 
2 CONC as 68. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was shortly after this time that Mattie, along with her two sisters, Anna and Julia, opened a dressmaking and millinery establishment in Northfield. They managed this business for several years. Their brother, Ole, operated a h
2 CONC otel where the girls stayed. There are stories told that these Lysne girls were known as “the belles of Northfield” in their time. They were blond haired, slender, fun living girls whom young men found very attractive.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was during this time that Mattie married Louis Solberg on December 23, 1875. They first lived in Minneapolis where Louis was a law enforcement officer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following children were born to Mattie and Louis while living in Minneapolis:
2 CONT *Lawerence ( January 17, 1876). He died June 8, 1877 and is buried alongside his father in the Layman Cemetery, Minneapolis, Minnesota. 
2 CONT *Alvilda (April 29, 1878). She was married to C. S. Englebrecht.
2 CONT *Ida Marie (March 18, 1881). She was married to Frank Wenger.
2 CONT *Milton (October 5, 1884). He lived in Escalon, California.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1900 federal census shows them living in the 5th Ward, Minneapolis. Included in the family are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Head 54 Foreman Wood Yard Mattie Wife 50 Alvilda Daughter 22 Stenographer Ida Marie Daughter 19 At home Oscar M. Son 15 At school
2 CONT
2 CONT Also listed is the year (1867) Louis emigrated to the United States, that he was naturalized and all members of the family could read, write, and understand English.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband’s death in 1907 Mattie continued to live in Minneapolis, apparently with her daughter and son-in-law, Frank Wenger The 1910 the federal census shows Mattie living in the 10th Ward, Minneapolis. Listed are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenger, Frank Head 39 Married Bookkeeper Grain Co. Ida Wife 29 Married Mildred Daughter 6 Single Lucille Daughter 2 Single Solberg, Mattie Mother-in-law 62 Widow Alvilda Sister-in-law 38 Single Milton Brother-in-law 25 Single
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1919, Mattie moved to Seattle, Washington, to live with her daughter, Alvilda, and son-in-law, Charles Englebrecht. The 1920 federal census shows the following people living together in a single household in Seattle:
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbrecht, Charles S. Head 60  Alvilda Wife 41 Solberg, Mattie Mother in law 71
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1930 federal census shows the family living in the same place. Listed are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbrecht, Alvilda Head 51 Widow Propriotor Lodging House Solberg, Mattie Mother 82 Widow Simpson, Norman Lodger 33 Married Copeland, Aubrey Lodger 25 Single
2 CONT
2 CONT Mattie died July 4, 1937, at the home of her daughter, Alvilda, at 4210 Burke Avenue, Seattle, Washington. She is buried in the Evergreen Cemetery, Seattle, Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
