2 CONT Washington Motorist article:
2 CONT AUTO CLUB GIVES PIONEER 50-YEAR CARD
2 CONT      Henry P. Grant, 76, who has driven for 50 years without a serious accident, has been awarded a 50 year membership card in the Autobmobile club of Washington, it was announced yesterday.  Grant, 15217 28th Av. S.W. is one of t
2 CONC he founders of the automobile club.
2 CONT      In 1903 Grant became Seattle's first automobile dealer as the operator of the Seattle Automobile Co.  He sold a one cylinder vehicle named the Thomas Flyer.  Later he sold Franklin Automobiles.
2 CONT      Grant also was one of the organizers of the Seattle Automobile Dealers; Association in 1907.  
2 CONT      Grant said he has been most impressed with the role motor vehicles have played in the growth of the Pacific Northwest.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT My Great-Grandfather, Henry P. Grant 1876-1954, was the first Auto Dealer in the state and a founding member of the Seattle Automobile club of Seattle.  There is an article on your site dealing with Ralph Hopkins and the first aut
2 CONC o in the state.  I thought that you might be interested in the following that was taken from a High School history paper that my Dad's Cousin wrote months before Henry Grant passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT      The first car was a Woods Electric owned by Ralph Hopkins; it looked like an open buggy.  Mr. Hopkins had bought it in Chicago for $1,400 and shipped it to Seattle by railroad.
2 CONT      Every morning as Mr. Hopkins drove his auto downtown from his home on First Hill he would stop at some busy street corner.  Gazing with wonder, a crowd of people would quickly assemble, examine all the parts, and ask all sort
2 CONC s of questions as to how it was propelled.  For Ralph, this was the time of his life, and he would proudly move on to another corner for a similar session.
2 CONT      Unfortunately, the Woods Electric began stopping at more inopportune times and places, and the mobs gathered around the auto to jeer, blockading traffic until Mr. Hopkins could get it fixed.  Seattle policemen clenched their 
2 CONC fists, gnashed their teeth, and became so fidgety that the only answer seemed to be to arrest Mr. Hopkins.
2 CONT      Between the threats of the police and the jeers of the multitudes, Mr. Hopkins did not lead a happy life, but hung on until he had company.
2 CONT      Ralph Hopkins once tried to drive his auto over the Lake Washington bicycle path.  The path, however, was too narrow and the car sunk hopelessly in the mud.  Henry Grant recalls this scene vividly, for he happened to come upo
2 CONC n the car and owner in this predicament--and it was his first sight of an automobile.
2 CONT      In 1902 Mr. Hopkins traded in his first automobile for a new Franklin.  This negotiation was the first automobile trade in Seattle.  Sometime later the Seattle Post-Intelligencer wanted a picture of the old Woods Electric and
2 CONC  had one taken with Mr. Grant at the wheel.  When Ralph saw the picture in the paper he came back to Mr. Grant and declared that he'd "just made fifty dollars playing poker and wanted to buy back the Woods Electric for sentimental
2 CONC  reasons."
2 CONT      Mr. Grant was surprised, but he laughed and said, "SOLD ! "  "Well," he explains, "the battery was gone--and those were expensive to replace, so I took the fifty dollars."
2 CONT      In 1920 Mr. Hopkins put the old buggy on display in the annual auto show.  Its final resting place is the State Historical Museum in Tacoma.  Mr. Grant tells how "a few years ago I visited the Historical Museum in Tacoma, and
2 CONC  to my astonishment I saw the Woods Electric in the basement--with Ezra Meeker's oxen.  One of the museum representatives was there by the car and I mentioned to him that I'd owned that car at one time.  He was glad to hear the hi
2 CONC story because the car had been given to the museum by a man who didn't know its story.  "Probably," Mr. Grant speculated, "the car just sat around for years after Ralph died.  It might have even changed hands, until finally someon
2 CONC e got it who recognized it only as a relic and so donated it to the museum."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: Washington Death Index 1907-1996
2 CONT Name: Henry P Grant 
2 CONT Place of Death: King 
