2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From History of King County by Bagley  P. 307-309
2 CONT Hon. Bennett O. Swain
2 CONT Endowed with the indomitable spirit and sterling qualities of his Norse ancestors, the Hon. Bennett O. Swain has made his own way in the world, steadily progressing toward the goal of success, and is now classed with the substanti
2 CONC al business men of Enumclaw.  He is a member of the state legislature and has demonstrated his public spirit by actual achievements for the general good.  He was born in Hancock, Minnesota, May 28, 1885, a son of Ole and Anna (Ber
2 CONC g) Swain, both natives of Vaage, Gudbransdalen, Norway.  The grandparents, John and Carrie Swain, always resided in that country and the former passed away on his farm in 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole Swain was born March 24, 1860, and acquired his education by attending night school.  Beginning his career as a wage earner when a boy of nine, he tended flocks of sheep and goats for five years and next became a tailor's appr
2 CONC entice.  He followed the trade for a time and also worked for a railroad in Norway.  In 1881 he followed the example of many of his fellow countrymen and came to the United States.  For one and a half years he was a farm hand in M
2 CONC innesota and afterward forked for the Canadian Pacific Railroad Company for two years.  Later he was engaged in railroad work in Iowa for a few months and next went to North Dakota, where he was employed on a ranch for one and a h
2 CONC alf years.  In 1888 he came to the Puget Sound district of Washington and for ten years thereafter was identified with construction activities in Seattle.  In 1898 he purchased a tract of forty acres on section thirty-two, situate
2 CONC d in the Neuwaukum district of King county between Auburn and Enumclaw, and comprising a portion of the John Jones homestead.  About four acres were under cultivation and a log cabin stood on the place.   Mr. Swain cleared the rem
2 CONC ainder of the land, which he brought to a high state of development, and improved the place with good buildings and neat, well kept fences.  At the time of his retirement he leased the land to his youngest son, who operates a dair
2 CONC y on the ranch, where the father still makes his home.  At the age of fourteen he was confirmed in the Lutheran church and throughout his life has conscientiously followed its teachings, thereby winning the esteem and friendship o
2 CONC f his associates.  In 1884 he was married in Breckenridge, Minnesota, to Miss Anna Berg, a daughter of Ole and Inga Berg, who were natives of Norway and passed away in Minnesota.  Mrs. Swain was reared in her native land, receivin
2 CONC g a good education, and came to the United States in 1880.  Eight children were born to Mr. And Mrs. Swain, namely:  Bennett O.; John, who is married and has a son, Frederick; Mr. Edna Anderson, who has one child, Ralph; Herbert M
2 CONC .; Harry, who is married and has a son, Richard; and Mrs. Gladys Pederson, who has one child, Marvin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bennett O. Swain attended the public schools of Seattle and a district school at Wabash, Washington.  Inheriting a love for the sea from his forbears, he became a sailor and for six years was employed on ships on the Atlantic and 
2 CONC Pacific oceans.  Afterward he was identified with the restaurant business in Dayton, Ohio, Detroit, Michigan, and other parts of the east and also in various cities in California and Washington.  In 1915 he purchased a ranch in Ki
2 CONC ng county and cleared about half of the place, which was covered with stumps.  He sold the property to advantage and for two years thereafter had charge of the Enumclaw business of Grange Warehouse Company.  Since 1921 Mr. Swain h
2 CONC as engaged in merchandising in Enumclaw under his own name and is the proprietor of one of its largest and finest grocery stores.  His enterprise, courtesy and honesty have been potent factors in the development of the business, w
2 CONC hich is efficiently managed.  In 1921 he built a large warehouse, connected by a spur track with the railroad which runs through Enumclaw, and was successfully engaged in the grain, hay, flour, feed, hardware and implement busines
2 CONC s until July 1, 1928, when he disposed of his hay and grain department.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Swain was married in 1907 to Miss Anna M. Grant, who was born in Minnesota and in infancy was taken to Seattle, where she received her education.  They have become the parents of a son, Clifford Grant, born January 4, 1917.  M
2 CONC r. Swain belongs to the local Kiwanis Club and in politics is a stanch democrat.  In 1927 he was chosen to represent the fortieth district in the state legislature of Washington.  To each question brought before the house he gave 
2 CONC earnest and thoughtful consideration and voted for those measures which he believed of benefit to his district and state.  He served on the following important committees in 1927-28 session; revenue and taxation, public utilities 
2 CONC and mines and mining.  He has never used public office as a means of personal aggrandizement and is a man of high principles and forceful personality, esteemed and respected by all with whom he has been associated in the varied re
2 CONC lations of life.
2 CONT
