2 CONT SEATTLE -- Boyd Smith, a Seattle Post-Intelligencer sports writer whose college basketball coverage earned him a national award, died at 89. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith died Friday after a stroke in San Jose, Calif. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired in 1980 after a 49-year career in which he also served for a time as president of the paper's newspaper guild local. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith covered Seattle University, which fielded national contenders at a time when the city had no big-league professional teams. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1958, when future NBA star Elgin Baylor led the Chieftains to the NCAA title game, Smith won the nation's top college sports writing award, presented by Notre Dame. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Obit:
2 CONT Monday, September 22, 2003
2 CONT
2 CONT Sportswriter loved basketball
2 CONT Former P-I staffer relished covering heyday of Seattle U
2 CONT
2 CONT By JAKE ELLISON
2 CONT SEATTLE POST-INTELLIGENCER REPORTER
2 CONT
2 CONT Boyd Smith, a former sports writer for the Seattle Post-Intelligencer, loved college basketball and loved writing about it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Boyd Smith died Friday after a stroke in San Jose, Calif. He was 89. 
2 CONT
2 CONT   In February 1980, when former P-I sportswriter Boyd Smith covered his last Seattle University game, the private Seattle college honored him at halftime with a lettermans jacket and a lifetime game pass. 
2 CONT Smith worked at the P-I for 49 years until his retirement in 1980. He started as a copyboy, running errands for reporters, but became well-known for his basketball coverage at a time when the city didn't have the SuperSonics and S
2 CONC eattle University supported one of the nation's top teams.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went to all the games, whether he was working or not, said his son, Paul Smith. And while he covered the University of Washington Huskies and Seattle Pacific Falcons, Smith loved covering SU's Chieftains the most. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the 1950s into the 1970s, SU had 20 consecutive winning seasons and was the home team for a number of basketball players who would go on to national stardom.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Shake the hand that shook the hand of the great Elgin Baylor," Smith sometimes urged his son, reaching out his hand as he came in the door when returning home from work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baylor played for SU from 1955 to 1958 and then played for the Lakers from 1958 to 1972, eventually becoming an NBA Hall of Fame inductee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon retiring, Smith was quoted in the P-I as saying the highlight of his career was "the two games Seattle U won down in San Francisco in 1958. Baylor hit about a 40-footer to beat USF in the first game and then the next night th
2 CONC e Chiefs beat California in overtime. That took them to the NCAA final four against top-ranked Kansas State, which had beaten out Wilt Chamberlain's Kansas team and Oscar Robertson's Cincinnati squad."
2 CONT
2 CONT SU won against Kansas but then lost to Kentucky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT That year, Smith's coverage won him the top college sportswriting award in the nation handed out by Notre Dame University.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1980, when Smith covered his last SU game, the private Seattle college honored him at halftime with a letterman's jacket and a lifetime game pass. 
2 CONT
2 CONT P-I staffers remembered Smith as hard-working and friendly, a man who could find humor even in a grim situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith's only grumble about work, his son said, surfaced the year he retired.
2 CONT
2 CONT "If he had any pet peeve," Paul Smith said, "it was the fact that they were converting to computers. He just loved his typewriter."
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith wasn't all basketball all the time. He was also an avid tennis player and would even meet his eldest son, who has since died, to help him practice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith also fought to unionize the P-I. In 1936, he walked out on strike until the workers achieved a union. He remained active in the guild and became its president in the late 1960s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in Hawaii during World War II. In Seattle after the war, he met and in 1947 married Ella, who was working at Boeing. Paul Smith said his parents loved dancing to bigband music and that passion accompanied them throughout
2 CONC  their marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT His dad always wore a tweed jacket and a plaid shirt to work, Paul Smith said, and he never owned a car. Instead, he relied on taxicabs, public transportation and often walked to work from his home on Queen Anne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his retirement, he kept up his walking habit, heading to the Pike Place Market several times a week for fresh vegetables and a stop at a tavern for a beer and sports talk. The Smiths owned their Queen Anne home from 1947 to 
2 CONC 2001, when Boyd Smith had a stroke that forced a move to San Jose to be near their son.
2 CONT
2 CONT "He was very well-known around Seattle," Paul Smith said. "I can remember that anywhere I went with him, people would come up and greet him."
2 CONT
2 CONT P-I reporter Jake Ellison can be reached at 206-448-8346 or jakeellison@seattlepi.com
2 CONT
